"Before printing, how were sutras passed on?": "4903f8c3daf4",
"Color every other square on the board.": "7754fceaff2a",
"Even after printing was invented, people went on copying sutras by hand, and many still do. The sutras themselves speak of writing them out as a good thing to do. For the people who copy, it is a way to show respect for the words, and a way to practice paying attention.": "6af39a9fb3b0",
"Imagine a world with no printers and no copy machines. If you wanted a book, someone had to write out every word by hand. For hundreds of years, that is how the Buddhist texts called sutras were passed on. Trained copyists wrote them on long rolls of paper with ruled columns, one small, neat character after another.": "1b6b2d53fa9a",
"Imagine a world with no printers and no copy machines. If you wanted a book, someone had to write out every word by hand. For hundreds of years, that is how the Buddhist texts called sutras were passed on. Trained copyists wrote them on long rolls of paper with ruled columns, one small, neat character after another. Even after printing was invented, people went on copying sutras by hand, and many still do. The sutras themselves speak of writing them out as a good thing to do. For the people who copy, it is a way to show respect for the words, and a way to practice paying attention. The rules are simple. Sit up straight. Write one character in each square. Start at the top of the column on the right, write down to the bottom, and then move one column to the left. Do not hurry. Try to make every character the same size, in the middle of its square. The most copied text is probably the Heart Sutra. It is very short: the translation by the Tang dynasty monk Xuanzang has only 260 characters. Its best-known line has eight characters: 色即是空，空即是色, “form is emptiness, emptiness is form.” People have thought about that line for more than a thousand years. In this lesson, we simply write it. One famous copyist was Master Hongyi. Before he became a monk in 1918, he was an art and music teacher named Li Shutong. As a monk he gave up most of his arts, but he kept on writing sutras and short sayings to give to people. He said that what his writing shows is calm and quiet. You do not have to be Buddhist to try the method. Monks in old Europe copied books by hand too, and so did scribes in many other traditions. Copying any text you care about, slowly and neatly, is good practice for the hand and for the mind.": "6ec8f04b668b",
